#!/usr/bin/python3
"""kiosk-shell -- the only window cage shows.

Two WebKit views in a stack:
  * "settings": kioskd's page (http://127.0.0.1:8088/). The first screen
                without an HMI address; the only view on the installer ISO.
  * "hmi":      the configured web HMI, fullscreen. Loaded only once its
                server answers (probe_hmi), then shown by itself.
Until then "connecting" shows ("Connecting…", drawn here as kiosk-splash
draws its bar), from the moment WebKit has loaded; an HMI that stops
answering or fails to load on screen gives way to it too (there is no
error page). The Settings corner, held ~1 s, opens Settings from it, and
then nothing switches by itself.

Separate views, not an <iframe>: HMI servers often forbid framing, and the
HMI never shares a JavaScript context with Settings. Settings talks back
through the "kiosk" script message handler.

Over the HMI (and "Connecting…"), a small update card in the Settings
corner (which stays above it, still held to open Settings; the card's
buttons on its far side) follows kioskd's updates (/api/update): a version
found and downloading, the download and install's progress, then "ready to
restart" with a hold-to-restart button. Each can be hidden; tapping the
card opens Settings → System. The same card, over any view and ahead of
the update, counts down kioskd's auto shutdown (the charger unplugged,
/api/power/auto), with Cancel.
"""

